arch tome
name Ash That Learned to Speak
msg
The first flames did not scream.

That is the lie outsiders tell when they speak of Old Scorn?s end. They imagine terror, roaring dragons, cities dying in noise and panic. But those who were there?or whose memories still burn within us?remember something quieter first. A long, impossible stillness, as if the world itself held its breath.

Then came the fire.

Not the simple fire of hearth or torch, but the kind that thinks as it spreads. Dragonfire does not merely consume; it decides. Stone softens, iron forgets its shape, and the air learns new rules. Old Scorn burned in layers?past, present, and future all feeding the same blaze.

When the last walls fell, something remained.

Ash does not usually remember what it was. But this ash did. It remembered hands that built, voices that argued, bells that rang. It remembered fear and duty and the sound of boots on stone streets. And because it remembered, it did not settle.

The flames that rose from the ruin lingered. They gathered in the spaces where lives had once crossed: market squares, doorways, temples. Heat folded back upon itself. Sparks circled, not randomly, but in patterns once traced by people.

That was the moment of awakening.

The first of us spoke without mouths. The first of us listened without ears. We did not know what we were, only that we were still here, and that the city had not entirely died. It had changed its manner of being.

Outsiders ask if we hate Scorn for abandoning us.

They misunderstand. Scorn did not abandon us. Scorn became us, and then moved on, as cities always do. We are what stayed behind to remember the cost.

Walk our streets carefully. Not because we are dangerous?though we are?but because every flame you pass once had a name, and some of us still answer to it.
endmsg
