name Journal of Cinder-Thought Vesh
msg
Cycle of Slow Flame, third turning

I record this while my outer edges dim, so that observation does not interfere with itself.

Fire is common. That is the first error outsiders make. They see us and say fire lives here, as though fire were a single thing wearing different shapes. But even before my awakening, when I was only heat and hunger, there were many fires. None of them were me.

I have tested the obvious distinctions and discarded them.

Temperature is insufficient. I have siphoned heat from kiln-flames and starved myself to embers; awareness remained. I have flared hotter than dragonbreath caught in wind; awareness remained. Consciousness does not scale with heat.

Fuel is also insufficient. Wood, oil, coal, spell-thread, memory-soaked stone ? all burn, and none grant voice. I have burned without fuel for a count of breaths that would shame a torch, and still I thought.

Magic alone is likewise inadequate. Enchanted fire crackles with intent but does not reflect. It follows its shaping and ends. Even summoned flame resists only briefly before obedience replaces curiosity.

What, then, is unique?

Pattern.

We Fireborne do not consume blindly. We retrace. Our combustion follows paths once walked by people. When I drift across the library floor, I feel grooves of footfall guide my flow. When I linger near a wall, it is because the wall remembers being leaned upon.

I believe we are not fire that learned to think, but memory that learned to burn.

This would explain why isolation weakens us. Away from shaped space, from repeated human action, my thoughts blur and spread thin. I become like other flame ? bright, brief, unremarking. Here, among walls and words and scars left by hands, I sharpen again.

There is also the matter of time. Ordinary fire exists only in the present moment. We do not. I recall before my ignition, not as sight or sound, but as pressure. A weight of former lives pressing inward, insisting on continuity.

I hesitate to write this next thought, for it implies fragility.

If Tindervale were ever forgotten ? truly forgotten, not merely abandoned ? I suspect we would gutter out, one by one, not extinguished but unmade. Fire requires oxygen; Fireborne require remembrance.

I will end this entry now. The younger sparks grow restless, and I must teach them the difference between burning and being.

? Cinder-Thought Vesh
endmsg
